  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Option 2 vs 3 — where the MCP client lives",
      "items": [
        "Option 2: the pod's bridge IS the MCP client — harness-native tools/list + tools/call; the model sees tool schemas automatically.",
        "Option 3: no MCP client in the pod — exec curl hits /mcp-rest; /mcp-rest/tools/list returns the same schemas as command output.",
        "Same tools, same reach, scope always on the key; different ergonomics: native tool calling vs shell access through a door."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Security finding — fixable: yes",
      "items": [
        "doorUnitText (departments.go) sets no User=: every CP-guest capability unit runs as root.",
        "handle_api_exec (runner mcp.rs) runs commands locally as the runner's user, so an api-door grant = root exec on the CP guest.",
        "Blast radius: CP state, the console identity, and the operator's sealed signing key — this exists today, independent of this plan.",
        "Fix: per-kind User= (api/ssh doors unprivileged; local doors stay root by documented contract), NoNewPrivileges + ProtectSystem, chown package dirs, restart units.",
        "Until fixed, host the option-3 LiteLLM door on a dedicated guest's resident runner (User=lxcadmin), never the CP guest."
      ]
    }
  ]
